8 #edb/ref 17592186087812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049540 #edb/ref 1031 "Finding Greatest Common Factors Using Prime Factorization" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049540 #edb/ref 1033 811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049540 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "fd04d2ec-50fb-5967-88af-eb531b17c1bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050526 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186051017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050526 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186133730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050526 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186133731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050526 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186133732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050526 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186133733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050526 #edb/ref 1031 "The Greatest Common Factor of Two Monomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050526 #edb/ref 1033 2181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050526 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "507c4552-c3af-55c9-9104-67d8bb5a0bd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1031 "Constructing Algebraic Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1033 3721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051009 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7931e951-f0f8-5132-97e2-67fa64802a65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186051017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1031 "Factoring Linear Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1033 87 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7f29909c-8f45-5a17-820a-13a0a327cd5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054489 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054489 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the $\\textrm{GCF}$ of $8a$, $4c$ and $12c$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054489 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7bc66df5-26b7-5eb0-b756-f8b086607209" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054489 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we decompose each expression into its separate factors. We express each constant as a product of its prime factors, and we treat each variable in the product as a separate factor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8a &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot a \\\\\n4c &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot c \\\\\n12c &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now match up the individual factors as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8a &= 2_(―) \\cdot 2_(―) \\cdot 2 \\cdot a \\\\\n4c &= 2_(―) \\cdot 2_(―) \\cdot c \\\\\n12c &= 2_(―) \\cdot 2_(―) \\cdot 3 \\cdot c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take one factor from each pair and multiply.\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(8a, 4c, 12c) = 2 \\cdot 2 = 4\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054494 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1603" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054494 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the $\\textrm{GCF}$ of $4a$ and $15b$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054494 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa1c4cbd-2086-5634-809f-43e4dd6becf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054494 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we decompose each expression into its separate factors. We express each constant as a product of its prime factors, and we treat each variable in the product as a separate factor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4a &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot a \\\\\n15b &= 3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we try to pair off the individual factors, we can't find any common factors. The only common factor is one. Thus,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(4a, 15b) = 1\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054588 #edb/ref 1069 "e-816" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054588 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the greatest common factor of $2x$ and $10y$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054588 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6ce77198-a3b2-5e9b-98d5-db8922addc1a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054588 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we decompose each expression into its separate factors. We express each constant as a product of its prime factors, and we treat each variable in the product as a separate factor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x &= 2 \\cdot x \\\\\n10y &= 2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we pair off the individual factors as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x &= 2_(―) \\cdot x \\\\\n10y &= 2_(―) \\cdot 5 \\cdot y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take one factor from each pair. Here, there is only one pair, so we just need to take that factor. There are no other factors to multiply it with.\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(2x,10y) = {\\color{red}{2}}\n$$\n\nThus, the greatest common factor of $2x$ and $10y$ is $2$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054589 #edb/ref 1069 "e-817" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054589 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the $\\textrm{GCF}$ of $16a$ and $4a$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054589 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f4d78520-1050-5ca1-a2da-1b813269dd9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054589 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we decompose each expression into its separate factors. We express each constant as a product of its prime factors, and we treat each variable in the product as a separate factor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16a &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot a \\\\\n4a &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we pair off the individual factors as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16a &= 2_(―) \\cdot 2_(―) \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot a_(―) \\\\\n4a &= 2_(―) \\cdot 2_(―) \\cdot a_(―)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take one factor from each pair and multiply.\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(16a, 4a) = \\underline{2} \\cdot \\underline{2} \\cdot \\underline{a} = 4a\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054687 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054687 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054687 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054687 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054687 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Greatest Common Factor when it is a Positive Constant" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054687 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186054588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054687 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "92176bb0-84dc-5fa4-8154-e974c05fff06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054688 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054688 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054688 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Greatest Common Factor when it Contains Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054688 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186054589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054688 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "01ac1e8c-128c-5c13-b845-105092ae74d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054756 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054756 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Greatest Common Factor of Three Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054756 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186054489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054756 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ee5e58b3-f6d7-56e2-bccd-97095e94495e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054766 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054766 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186055401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054766 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Greatest Common Factor when there are No Common Factors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054766 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186054494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054766 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "872ca3cb-830c-5af5-92f1-230611a8a693" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054927 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054927 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054927 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054927 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054927 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054927 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054927 #edb/ref 1045 "The Greatest Common Factor of Two Linear Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054927 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186055656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054927 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054927 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "0c86ff1c-1fc9-55e7-bf07-5e59726ce644" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055656 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055656 #edb/ref 1061 2779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055656 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c78d0b7d-678e-5655-a213-713eadefdc2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055656 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186055936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055657 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055657 #edb/ref 1061 2780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055657 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ed6a9f50-301e-58d1-8a60-afe81d9aaa27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055657 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055658 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055658 #edb/ref 1061 2784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055658 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0a66a45e-e56e-5d68-a099-199219a3f0cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055658 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055763 #edb/ref 1061 4767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055763 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "13969cc3-8838-5efa-96ad-09b1b4177c3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055763 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055777 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055777 #edb/ref 1061 5150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055777 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "88a2f431-9a52-5f66-9911-286921d1d2ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055777 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055936 #edb/ref 1064 "The **greatest common factor** (or $\\textrm{GCF}$) of $12$ and $18x$ is the largest expression that will divide *both* $12$ and $18x$ with no remainder.\n\nTo find the GCF, we first decompose each expression into its separate factors. We express each constant as a product of its prime factors, and we treat each variable in the product as a separate factor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n18x &= 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we pair off the individual factors as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12 &= 2_(―) \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3_(―) \\\\\n18x &= 2_(―) \\cdot 3_(―) \\cdot 3 \\cdot x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take one factor from each pair and multiply.\n\n$$\n\\textrm{GCF}(12,18x) = {\\color{red}2} \\cdot {\\color{blue}3} = 6\n$$\n\nThus, the greatest common factor of $12$ and $18x$ is $6$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055936 #edb/ref 1065 1360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055936 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b2b71877-6097-5d59-a2b8-7440bd0d5329" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055936 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
